i='TCS-3457';_,c=card(i);refs=c['references'];refs[0]['locator']='Section 1.1, Theorem 2, Lemma 42 and Section 6, second open question.'
refs.extend([
dict(id='local2025',title='Probabilistic Guarantees to Explicit Constructions: Local Properties of Linear Codes',authors='Fernando Granha Jeronimo; Nikhil Shagrithaya',year=2025,url='https://eccc.weizmann.ac.il/report/2025/158/',locator='Revision 1, 19 November 2025: Remark 1.4 and Corollary 6.13 (erasure list recovery), enlarged constant alphabet.'),
dict(id='ensemble2025',title='Optimal Erasure Codes and Codes on Graphs',authors='Yeyuan Chen; Mahdi Cheraghchi; Nikhil Shagrithaya',year=2025,url='https://arxiv.org/abs/2504.03090',locator='Theorem 1 and Definition 13: for each erasure pattern, most members of an explicit ensemble decode it.')])
issue='The source does not specify the intended linear-code rate R(τ), list bound L(τ), admissible dependence of τ on message or block length, or exact uniform construction resource bound. Its nonlinear rate/list pair cannot simply be demanded of linear codes. These tradeoffs, rather than just the word near-optimal, must define the benchmark.'
notes=['Recovered binary linear erasure list decoding with adversarial coordinate sets, rate and list size.','Defined the generator-matrix rank criterion and distinguished erasures from errors.','Preserved the unspecified rate/list tradeoff and parameter regime; did not import the impossible nonlinear list-size goal.','Separated the 2025 larger-alphabet construction and per-pattern ensemble guarantee from a single binary code handling every pattern.']
save(i,dict(title='Near-optimal explicit binary linear codes for erasure list decoding',formal='Construct explicit binary linear codes with near-optimal rate and list size for adversarial erasure list decoding. Concretely, for a surviving fraction 0<τ≤1 and block length N, a code C≤F₂^N of dimension K should have at most L codewords consistent with every assignment of binary values to any set of at least ⌈τN⌉ coordinates; its rate is R=K/N. The source asks for near-optimal linear codes after constructing nonlinear ones, but does not specify the required functions R(τ), L(τ), or the regime relating τ and N. It therefore does not yet state one quantitative construction theorem.',definitions='F₂={0,1} with arithmetic modulo 2. A binary linear [N,K] code is the image of an injective linear encoding m↦mG, where m∈F₂^K and the K×N generator matrix G has rank K. Its block length is N and rate is K/N. For T⊆[N] and z∈F₂^T, let C(T,z)={c∈C: c|_T=z}. Erasure list decoding with surviving fraction τ and maximum list size L means |C(T,z)|≤L for every T with |T|≥⌈τN⌉ and every z. The erased coordinates are known to the decoder; surviving values must match exactly. The guarantee is worst-case over coordinate sets and values, not a probability over random erasures. If G_T is the submatrix of columns indexed by T, then every nonempty C(T,z) has exactly 2^{K−rank(G_T)} elements, so the property is equivalent to 2^{K−rank(G_T)}≤L for all such T. It is enough to check |T|=⌈τN⌉ because revealing more coordinates cannot increase the list. L is a positive integer. The source’s explicitness concerns encoding, whereas a separate concluding question concerns efficient decoding of its nonlinear construction. For a linear code, a displayed generator matrix permits encoding by binary matrix multiplication; an explicit family still needs a uniform algorithm to produce or access its generators with a stated resource bound. No such near-optimal family specification is fixed by the extracted sentence.',question_type='yes_no',answer_criterion='After fixing admissible N,τ and the required rate, list bound and construction time, provide one uniform binary-linear code construction meeting those guarantees for every admissible parameter choice, with proofs; or prove that the specified conjunction is impossible. The source passage currently leaves those quantitative choices unresolved. A larger-alphabet code, a nonlinear code, or an ensemble whose successful member depends on the erasure pattern does not meet the same single-code binary-linear requirement.',context_blocks=[dict(title='What the 2020 nonlinear result achieves',text='For every fixed 0<γ<1 and an inverse-polynomial surviving fraction in its message-length regime, the source constructs an explicitly encodable binary code of rate τ^{1+γ} and list size log^{O_γ(1)}(1/τ). This is an erasure result, not the corresponding adversarial bit-error problem.',citation='primary'),dict(title='Linearity changes the attainable list size',text='The paper records Guruswami’s Ω(1/τ) list-size obstruction for the linear-code regime under discussion and stresses that its much smaller lists use nonlinearity. Its request for near-optimal linear codes must therefore be judged against linear-code tradeoffs, not by copying the nonlinear parameter pair.',citation='primary'),dict(title='Explicit construction does not automatically preserve a binary alphabet',text='A November 2025 preprint gives explicit erasure-list-recovery constructions with rate and list parameters comparable to random linear codes, but enlarges the alphabet by a parameter-dependent amount. The alphabet remains constant with block length; that does not make it binary. Corollary 6.13 in the checked revision is the relevant erasure result.',citation='local2025'),dict(title='Ensembles have a different quantifier order',text='Another 2025 preprint constructs constant-size ensembles over any fixed alphabet near the Singleton rate. For each erasure pattern, most codes in the ensemble correct it. This does not assert that one member corrects every pattern, or give the requested small-list property for a single binary code.',citation='ensemble2025')],why='Explicit binary linear codes with sharp erasure tradeoffs would combine simple algebraic encoding with strong guarantees against adversarial information loss. The question also tests the cost of linearity relative to the source’s nonlinear constructions.',references=refs,progress=[dict(date='2020',text='The nonlinear construction achieves the stated near-linear rate exponent and polylogarithmic lists, and explicitly leaves the linear-code question open.',citation='primary'),dict(date='2025',text='New explicit constructions address local code properties over enlarged alphabets, while a separate result obtains strong per-pattern guarantees from ensembles. Their models differ from this binary single-code target.',citation='local2025')],status='uncertain',status_note='Read the 2020 model, rank criterion, theorem and exact question; checked the November 2025 local-property revision and the erasure-ensemble theorem. Searches through 12 September 2026 found related list-recovery and larger-alphabet work but no source-specified numerical target that can be certified as solved or still open here.',model_self_contained=False,requires_context=True,statement_review=dict(status='needs_specification',reviewed_on=DATE,notes=notes,remaining_issue=issue),review_note=issue,importance=dict(score=c['importance']['score'],method='editorial',reason='An explicit coding tradeoff with adversarial erasures, binary alphabets and a meaningful linear/nonlinear distinction; existing score retained.',assessed_on=DATE),working_summary=dict(sentences=['The target is a single explicit binary linear code that keeps only a small list of possible messages after adversarial erasures.','Its parameters are the surviving coordinate fraction, encoding rate and maximum list size.','The source’s nonlinear construction achieves a list-size advantage that cannot simply be required of linear codes.','It does not specify the desired linear rate/list tradeoff or the full parameter and construction regime.','Later larger-alphabet and code-ensemble results are recorded with their different guarantees, while the missing benchmark choices remain explicit.'],basis='saved_sources',source_basis='source-text',written_on=DATE,source_refs=['primary','local2025','ensemble2025'])),notes=notes,checked_sources=[r['url'] for r in refs],state='improved_unfinished')
